From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Send the selected output width with its depth limits

Keep the output selection and depth rules unchanged. Version 6 sends
output_width immediately before depth_limits, including on an output or
pixel-width change with equal limits. Older resources keep their existing
events. The library owns the buffer-pixel conversion.

Assisted-by: GPT-6 (Codex)

--- a/src/wayland/protocols/stereo/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
+++ b/src/wayland/protocols/stereo/kde-stereo-content-v1.xml
@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@
     SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT-CMU
     ]]></copyright>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_content_manager_v1" version="5">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_content_manager_v1" version="6">
   <description summary="declare stereo content of surfaces">
     A program tells the compositor that a surface's picture holds two
     views packed full side by side, with the left view first.
@@ -52,7 +52,7 @@
   </request>
 </interface>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_content_v1" version="5">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_content_v1" version="6">
   <description summary="stereo content declaration of one surface">
     The declaration of one surface, created with
     kde_stereo_content_manager_v1.create.
@@ -125,7 +125,7 @@
   </request>
 </interface>
 
-<interface name="kde_stereo_depth_v1" version="5">
+<interface name="kde_stereo_depth_v1" version="6">
   <description summary="desktop depth of one surface">
     The compositor shows the desktop's own surfaces at depth levels: the
     active window at the screen, the windows lying behind it sunk, menus,
@@ -193,23 +193,7 @@
     <arg name="output_edges" type="uint" enum="edge" summary="the surface's edges on its output's edge"/>
   </event>
 
-  <event name="depth_limits" since="5">
-    <description summary="The depth limits on the surface's output">
-      Sent after depth_state once the object is created and again whenever
-      either value changes, such as when the surface moves to another output,
-      the output's mode or kind changes, or the user changes the settings.
-
-      sunk and popped are how far behind and in front of the screen the
-      compositor lets anything go on the output the surface mainly sits on,
-      in pixels of a view 1920 wide. They are the user's limits built on the
-      output's display: a headset gets more separation, a framed 3D screen
-      less (the display's kind and physical size, libstereo-policy). A
-      surface takes them as they are for its screen, in its buffer's pixels
-      through stereo_depth_limits_for_buffer, never by its own width.
-    </description>
-    <arg name="sunk" type="fixed" summary="the sunk limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
-    <arg name="popped" type="fixed" summary="the popped limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
-  </event>
+
 
   <request name="set_desktop_depth">
     <description summary="Mark the surface's pair as the desktop's own depth">
@@ -249,6 +233,45 @@
       wl_surface commit.
     </description>
   </request>
+
+  <event name="depth_limits" since="5">
+    <description summary="The depth limits on the surface's output">
+      Sent after depth_state once the object is created and again whenever
+      either value changes, such as when the surface moves to another output,
+      the output's mode or kind changes, or the user changes the settings.
+
+      sunk and popped are how far behind and in front of the screen the
+      compositor lets anything go on the output the surface mainly sits on,
+      in pixels of a view 1920 wide. They are the user's limits built on the
+      output's display: a headset gets more separation, a framed 3D screen
+      less (the display's kind and physical size, libstereo-policy). A
+      surface uses the limits for that screen through the shared
+      stereo_depth_limits_for_buffer conversion, never by its own width.
+      Since version 6, output_width is sent immediately before every
+      depth_limits event. Together they describe the same selected output.
+      The compositor sends both again when that output or its pixel width
+      changes, even when sunk and popped stay the same.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="sunk" type="fixed" summary="the sunk limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+    <arg name="popped" type="fixed" summary="the popped limit, pixels of a view 1920 wide"/>
+  </event>
+
+  <event name="output_width" since="6">
+    <description summary="Pixel width of the output the limits are for">
+      Sent immediately before each depth_limits event to resources of
+      version 6 or newer. The width is the pixel width of one view of the
+      output the surface mainly sits on, the same output as the following
+      limits. It is independent of the surface, window and buffer sizes.
+      Zero means the surface has no selected output with a known pixel width.
+
+      One separation setting, centralized, tailored to the screen, not to
+      the element size: the person's choice, built on the display. Clients
+      pass this width and the following limits to the shared buffer-pixel
+      conversion; they never rescale depth by their own window's width.
+    </description>
+    <arg name="width" type="uint" summary="selected output width in pixels per view, or zero"/>
+  </event>
+
 </interface>
 
 </protocol>
--- a/src/scene/stereodepthstates.cpp
+++ b/src/scene/stereodepthstates.cpp
@@ -106,9 +106,12 @@
         // the limits of the output the window mainly sits on: the person's, built on that display
         const qreal sunk = StereoDepth::sunkLimit(window->output());
         const qreal popped = StereoDepth::poppedLimit(window->output());
-        surface->traverseTree([awake, edges, sunk, popped](SurfaceInterface *child) {
+        const LogicalOutput *output = window->output();
+        const uint32_t outputWidth = output ? output->pixelSize().width() : 0;
+        const QUuid outputId = output ? output->backendOutput()->uuid() : QUuid();
+        surface->traverseTree([awake, edges, sunk, popped, outputWidth, outputId](SurfaceInterface *child) {
             if (child->followsStereoDepth()) {
-                child->setStereoDepthState(awake, edges, sunk, popped);
+                child->setStereoDepthState(awake, edges, sunk, popped, outputWidth, outputId);
             }
         });
     }
